2. Structure of wall-bounded turbulent flows
2.1. Mean flow over smooth walls
Turbulent flows are irregular in nature, and the variations from one instant to another is substantial; however, the bulk flow can be described by mean flow characteristics. Any expressions and law for the description of the mean flow must, not only express the mean flow pattern but also satisfy the non-slip boundary condition at the wall and the condition of the zero-slope of velocity at the edge of the boundary layer. The bulk properties of mean flow in both smooth- and rough-wall boundary layers can be derived by classical asymptotic matching process [21,84,85]. Let δ be the thickness of boundary layer and u* the friction velocity (
τw/ρ
) (with τw the wall shear stress and ρ the fluid density). Assume also the flow is in the “equilibrium” state, in which δ and u* have a sufficient slow variation in the streamwise direction x. Therefore, both δ and u* can be considered as local scale at any particular x direction. The asymptotic matching analysis suggests that the boundary layer (over both smooth and rough walls) comprises of two overlapping regions; an outer layer scaling with u* and δ, and an inner layer scaling with u* and a set of length scales characterising the surface. For smooth surfaces, the set of length scales includes just the viscous length scale ν/u*, while, for rough surfaces, it consists of ν/u* in addition to all length scales required to completely characterise the roughness (e.g. roughness height, kurtosis, skewness, etc.).
The damping effect of the wall alters the regime of the turbulent flow close to the wall and leads to the formation of the boundary layer flow over the wall into several distinct layers. Fig. 3 illustrates the variation of the dimensionless mean velocity profile of the turbulent boundary layer with the logarithmic distance from the wall. The turbulent boundary layer consists of an “inner layer” which is attached to the wall and the “outer layer” that is generated above the inner layer. The inner layer is associated with the active region of turbulent flow that is affected by viscosity and local flow parameters. In contrast, the outer layer is associated with the inactive turbulent region that is influenced by the wall geometry. The inner region itself consists of three subsequence layers: “viscous (laminar) sublayer”, “buffer layer”, and “logarithmic layer”.
