The aptly named log-layer region comprises a natural logarithmic velocity distribution, which is referred to as the “law of the wall” (also termed as “log-law”) and expressed as:
(2)
U+=1κln(y+)+C,
where κ is the von Karman constant (κ ≈ 0.41), and the value of constant C is approximately 5.1. The log-layer is a fully turbulent layer in which the Reynolds shear stress greatly exceeds the viscous shear stress. There is a transition layer, known as the “buffer layer” between viscous sublayer and log layer where viscous shear stress almost equals the Reynolds shear stress (
−ρu′v′¯)
. The buffer layer is characterised by a high-level turbulent mixing due to turbulent production in this region and increased turbulence intensity. The log-layer region is also characterised by turbulence intensity and production, but to a lesser degree, since it has a smaller velocity gradient compared to the buffer layer.
The logarithmic law is not applicable in the outer layer (y+ > 700) since the measured turbulent velocity exceeds the predicted values. The outer layer is unaffected by the direct influence of the wall, which extended to 70% or 80% of the total thickness of the boundary layer. In this region, free shear layer prevails, and the velocity profile deviates considerably from the logarithmic form. The outer layer is also called the defect layer because there is some apparent velocity defect with respect to the free-stream velocity.
In order to cover the defect layer, Coles [90] suggested that the standard velocity profile of a turbulent boundary layer, with the linear and logarithmic sublayer, could be modified with an additional wake function that comes into effect gradually as the outer edge of the boundary layer approaches. The Coles’ “law-of-the-wake” is written as
(3)
U+=1κln(y+)+C+Π(x)κω(yδ).
Coles’ “law-of-the-wake” is a superposition of the law-of-the-wall due to the wall shear stress and the law of the wake due to the free turbulence at the core. Wake functions ω identify how wake modification emerges with increasing y. Coles [90] expressed the wake function numerically, and [91] approximated it as a sine-squared function.
(4)
ω(yδ)=2Πκ(sinπ.y2δ)2.
The Π function represents the free turbulence effect on the mean velocity, which may vary along the streamwise direction x. The Π function depends on the pressure gradient and describes how retarded the boundary layer is and how well-developed the wake region is. Usually, the wake function is normalized such that ω(1) = 2, and thus the Π function characterizes the strength of the wake in terms of the deviation from the log law at y = δ [92,93]. Chauhan et al. [92] suggested a composite profile fit comprising exponential functions that enable calculations of δ and Π for boundary layer. The adapted versions of these profiles were employed by Nagib & Chauhan [94] for pipe and channel flows. An alternative versions of the law-of-the-wake have been proposed by several researchers [95], [96], [97], [98], [99], [100], [101], [102], [103], [104].
The simple law-of-the-wall (the log-law) does not satisfy the no-slip boundary condition at the wall and the condition of zero-slope of the velocity profile at the outer edge of the boundary layer. The law-of-the-wake also does not satisfy the no-slip boundary condition. Spalding [105] proposed a single equation that covers all four layers (viscous, buffer, log law, and defect layers) and has reported excellent agreement with experimental data in the range of 0 ≤ y+ ≤ 2000 [106,107]. The Spalding law-of-the-wall is expressed as
(5)
y+=U++exp(−κc)[exp(κU+)−1−∑n=14(κU+)nn!]for0≤y+≤2000.
where the recommended values for κ and c are 0.4 and 5.5, respectively.
Reichardt [108] suggested a correlation for the mean velocity profile of based on the turbulent flow data in pipe and channels, which represent the velocity distribution close to the wall as well as in the core of flow, expressed as
(6)
U+=1κln(1+κy+)+7.8[1−exp(−y+11)−(y+11)exp(−0.33y+)]
The Spalding law and Reichardt law can be applied to predict incompressible turbulent flow without adverse pressure gradient and separation. Moreover, both of them satisfy the no-slip condition; however, the zero slope of velocity profile at the outer edge of the boundary layer is not fulfilled, as shown in Fig. 3. Kestin and Richardson [109] summarise the mean velocity profile in the inner region of the turbulent boundary layer.
Turbulent heat transfer over smooth surfaces can be determined by the Reynolds analogy. Based on the similarity between hydraulic and thermal boundary layer, Reynolds [110] postulates the existence of an analogy between wall shear stress, τw,and heat flux, qw. More discussion on the Reynolds analogy and temperature profile over smooth walls can be found in Bejan [111] and Kays and Crawford [78].
Several hypotheses have been developed to explain the structure of the turbulent boundary layer. Ludwig Prandtl hypothesised the first theory in this field, which is referred to as Prandtl's mixing length theory [113]. The mixing length theory states that the horizontal component of mean flow is proportional to the logarithm of height. Prandtl's mixing length theory assumes that the fluid fluctuations can be simplified and displaced by a distance
l
, known as Prandtl's mixing length, perpendicular to the main flow direction. Accordingly, the turbulence model of the mixing length can be described by the following equation [76]
(7)
τt=ρℓ2|∂U∂y|∂U∂y,
where τt is turbulent shear stress which is a model of the shear stress created by turbulent fluctuations. Subsequent to Prandtl's mixing length theory, two important hypotheses, the Monin–Obukhov (M-O) similarity hypothesis, and Townsend hypothesis were developed and are now discussed in the following sections.
2.2. Monin–Obukhov (M-O) similarity
The M-O Similarity was originally proposed based on the important understanding of Atmospheric Boundary Layer (ABL) flows. This hypothesis describes the atmospheric boundary layer by Monin–Obukhov (also referred as to Obukhov) length scale, L, expressed as [114], [115], [116], [117], [118], [119]
(8)
L=−(U*)3κ(gTw)(qρcp),
where κ is the von Karman constant and U* is friction velocity and g is gravity acceleration, while Tw, q,  ρ, and cp are the wall temperature, kinematic heat flux, fluid density, and fluid heat capacity, respectively. M-O similarity stated that the local variable of turbulent flow could be characterised by “universal functions”. In accordance with the Monin–Obukhov dimensionless parameter ξ = y/L (where y is the direction normal to the surface), the velocity and temperature profile can be expressed as [120,121]:
(9)
(κyU*)∂U∂y=φm(ξ);
(10)
(yTw)∂T∂y=φH(ξ);
where U and T are mean velocity and mean temperature profiles, respectively, while ϕm and ϕH are momentum and heat exchange universal functions, respectively. Based on experimental observations, several universal functions have been developed in the literature [122], [123], [124], [125], [126], [127], [128], [129]. More discussions on the universal functions can be found in Maronga and Reuder [130], Blaunshtein and Kopeika [121], and Foken [120]. Fig. 4 compares some of the universal functions reported in distinctive studies in the literature and demonstrates that universal functions proposed by different authors are in agreement for ξ <  0 (unstable flow stratification condition) but not for ξ >  0 (stable flow stratification conditions).
