The wall similarity hypothesis (universal velocity defect law) has been supported by numerous studies with different types of roughness [224,[226], [227], [228], [229], [230], [231], [232], [233], [234], [235], [236], [237], [238], [239], [240]]. The validity of wall similarity hypothesis and defect velocity law has also been verified by numerous experimental studies performed by Karen A. Flack and Michae P. Schultz and their colleagues [82,223,[235], [236], [237],[241], [242], [243], [244], [245], [246], [247], [248], [249], [250], [251]] and also Kenneth T. Christensen and his colleagues [87,224,225,[252], [253], [254], [255], [256], [257]]. Flack and Schultz [250] outline their 15 years of experimental research on rough-wall turbulent flows, where they reported the observation of wall similarity hypothesis in all their experiments. They also demonstrated that similarity in the outer layer is also observed in the quadrant-decomposed components of Reynolds stress (
