4.3. “k-type” and “d-type” roughness
The terms k-type and d-type roughness are characteristics used for two-dimensional roughness such as spanwise ribs and grooves. The distinction between k-type and d-type roughness was initially suggested by Perry et al. [295], who observed that for some type of surface roughness the flow behaviour is similar to the usual Clauser or Nikuradse scheme; that is the rough-wall friction factor (f) - Reynolds number (Re) curve for constant Ue is identical to that of smooth walls for constant U (the same concept as that in the wall similarity hypothesis). They also stated that the resulting graph of U+versus kU*/ν is universal for a given roughness geometry and gives the relationship for both transitionally and fully rough flow regimes in terms of variables required for boundary layer analysis. However, Streeter & Chu [296] and Ambrose [297] have reported examples of different behaviour of flow over rough surfaces, whose friction factor-Reynolds number characteristics are sensitive to relative scale k/d. This type of roughness is characterised by a smooth surface with a series of depression or grooves which the outer flow generate stable vortices. In this case, as Perry et al. [295] showed, U+ is a function of the product
Ref
 alone which is proportional to dU*/ν instead of kU*/ν. Since the characteristic length scale d was used for describing the roughness function, this result appears to be inconsistent with “law of the wall” for rough-wall flows developed by Nikuradse [287] and Clauser [298]. Based on these observations, Perry et al. [295] proposed two types of roughness, k-type and d-type, while the symbols denoting the significant length scale involved. They summarised previous evidence for d-type roughness behaviour and described the related mean velocity profile over d-type roughness. Basically, the d-type roughness is terminology for tightly packed ribs/grooves, while widely spaced ribs/grooves are of k-type roughness. The k-type two-dimensional roughness behaves similarly to the random three-dimensional roughness.
In a follow on from earlier works in this field, Coleman et al. [299] performed experiments of flow structures within the cavities between consecutive ribs of uniform cross-section. They outlined three distinctive types of roughness (Fig. 11) based on the pitch ratio L/H: the d-type, the intermediate type, and the k-type. Here L is the distance between ribs and H is the height of ribs.
