6.1. Mean velocity profile over rough walls
Clauser [292,298] studied the effect of the surface roughness on the mean velocity distribution and presented a method that has demonstrated to be robust and widely applied. He argued that the velocity profile in the viscous sublayer for rough surfaces follows a logarithmic pattern with a similar slope as for smooth surfaces, as illustrated in Fig. 12. He also found that the sole impact of the roughness is a downward shift of the log-law profile as a function of the roughness Reynolds number, as illustrated in Fig. 12. Clauser [298] and Hama [332] each in an independent study introduced a parameter called roughness function (ΔU+) to emulate the effect of the velocity shift caused by surface roughness. They observed that the surface roughness shifts the logarithmic profile downwards, commensurate with ΔU+, indicating a shortage in the momentum compared to smooth walls. The velocity shift is proportional to
lnks+
 [333] and is independent of wall distance [334]. The shift velocity and roughness function is also a result of wall similarity for characterizing the roughness effect on the mean flow. These have been experimentally validated with numerous experiments including the works of Karen A. Flack and Michael P. Schultz and their colleagues [82,223,[235], [236], [237],[241], [242], [243], [244], [245], [246], [247], [248], [249], [250], [251]] and also Kenneth T. Christensen and his colleagues [87,224,225,[252], [253], [254], [255], [256], [257]] as well as recent DNS studies [[71], [72], [73],[258], [259], [260]].
The equilibrium boundary layer flow over rough walls comprises two overlapping regions; an outer layer scaling with u* and δ, and an inner layer scaling with ν/u* and all length scales required to thoroughly characterise roughness (e.g. roughness height, kurtosis, skewness, etc.). It is evident that the velocity profile also depends on the distance from the wall y itself. However, the definition of the origin of y for rough walls requires careful attention, since the roughness itself dislocates the entire flow in y-direction normal to the wall. To account for this, the displaced height is defined as Y = y − d, where d is the “fluid dynamic height origin” or “zero-plane displacement” depending on both flow and roughness characteristics.
Clauser [298] proposed the logarithmic velocity distribution for turbulent flow over rough walls as
(25)
U+=1κlny++C−ΔU+;
where C is the smooth-wall intercept and κ is the von Kármán constant. He also asserted that the roughness function ΔU+ is a sole function of roughness Reynolds number
ks+
.
Hama [332] performed an extensive experimental study and proposed the roughness function for fully rough flows as
(26)
ΔU+=1κlnks++C−B;
Schlichting [76] proved that B is a constant value of 8.5 for fully rough flow regime in pipes. A similar value was reported by Pimenta et al. [335] for fully rough boundary layer over uniform sphere roughness. The relationship proposed for velocity profile by Clauser [298] and the roughness function by Hama [332] are both universal for given roughness geometry in pipes, channels, and zero pressure gradient boundary layer flows. Perry & Joubert [336] demonstrated that this universality extends to boundary layer flows in adverse pressure gradients.
The relationship proposed by Clauser [298] and Hama [332] are confined to the inner layer. By using Coles’ “law-of-the-wake” [90], the correlation can be extended to cover both the overlap and outer region of the boundary layer as [90]
(27)
U+=1κlny++C−ΔU++Π(x)κw(yδ);
Flack and Schultz [250] in a review of their 15 years of experimental research of turbulent flow on rough surfaces stated that since no “universal” roughness function exists, no single roughness length scale can characterize all roughness types in all roughness regimes. They also showed that in the fully rough regime, the hydraulic roughness length scale is related to the root-mean-square roughness height and skewness; however, the onset of roughness effects could be associated with the largest surface features which are characterised by the peak-to-valley height. Moreover, the behaviour of roughness function in the transitionally rough regime is complicated and varies significantly between roughness types.
ABL flow over rough terrains such as plant and urban canopies is classified as fully rough since the roughness elements (obstacles) are so large that the laminar sublayer is completely destroyed and the flow is considered to be independent of the molecular viscosity [337]. In ABL flow analysis, the roughness sublayer is generally represented by an aerodynamic roughness length scale y0, which matches the smooth wall log-law profile, thus the velocity profile for rough walls reads as [21,338]
(28)
U+=1κln(y−dy0);
where d is zero-plane displacement which is required to fix the origin of y. Basically, d is a fluid dynamic property that represents the mean level of momentum absorption by the rough surface [21]. More discussion on calculation of zero-plane displacement d can be found elsewhere [21,233,295,339]. Eq. (28) is only valid in the fully rough regime; therefore, the hydraulic (or aerodynamic) roughness length y0 is an alternative scale that is similar to the equivalent sand-grain roughness height ks, imposing collapse to a single profile in the fully rough regime and can be set to y0 = ks/32.6 as suggested by Schlichting [3].
6.2. Roughness functions
The roughness function (ΔU+) emulates the effect of the velocity shift caused by surface roughness as a downward shift to the log-law profile. Roughness functions suggested in the literature have been correlated based on the collapse to either traditional Nikuradse [287,288] data with three distinctive functions for each roughness regimes (referred as to “Nikuradse-type” roughness function) or Colebrook et al. [340] data with single expression function (referred as to “Colebrook-type” roughness function).
In the hydraulically smooth regime, the perturbations caused by roughness elements are completely damped out by molecular viscosity, resulting in that ΔU+ = 0. In the transitionally rough regime, with increasing
ks+
